 % Natural language processing provides the closest precedents for learning failure signals from Transformer attention graphs. Earlier approaches construct handcrafted structural descriptors: \citet{Kushnareva2021} use topological features of attention maps for artificial-text detection, while \citet{Binkowski2025} use spectral features of attention graphs for hallucination detection. HalluZig \citep{Samaga2026} models the evolution of attention-graph topology across layers using zigzag persistence. More recent approaches learn directly from attributed token graphs. HaluGNN \citep{Kong2026b} uses hidden states as node features and attention values as weighted edges, while CHARM \citep{Frasca2026} jointly represents activations and attention in a token graph and applies edge-aware message passing; its ablations demonstrate an advantage of preserving graph connectivity over a corresponding set-based model. CausalGaze \citep{Kong2026a} further refines attention graphs using gradient-guided counterfactual sensitivity of attention edges. To our knowledge, no prior work applies graph-based message passing to per-image ViT patch-attention graphs for predicting visual classification failures. Polygraph studies this vision-specific setting and, through matched non-graph and rewiring ablations, explicitly evaluates how much predictive value is attributable to graph structure itself.
 
+We extend these findings with controlled follow-ups testing competitiveness against a fixed LogitDynamics adaptation, score complementarity, and graph specificity. These studies use distinct development protocols and complement the original non-graph controls.
